Not quite; it includes the cost of reading a non-constant global. BenchmarkTools evaluates the expression in global scope, so `x` is looked up and type-checked each call. Interpolate it with `$` so the value is treated as a local:

```julia
using BenchmarkTools

x = 0.5
@btime sin(x)          # includes global-variable access
@btime sin($x)         # measures sin on a Float64 value
@btime sin(0.5)        # the literal can be constant-folded away: not a real measurement
```

In my runs the global version was a few times slower than the `$x` version on this tiny kernel, and the literal was a few times faster than `$x` because the compiler folded the call. For a kernel that takes microseconds the global overhead disappears into the noise, but for nanosecond kernels it dominates.

Rule of thumb: interpolate every input with `$`, and don't pass literal constants to a function you want to time.

Assert the inferred return type with `@inferred` and assert allocations after a warm-up call. Both run in the normal test suite and both fail with a precise message:

```julia
using Test, InteractiveUtils

function normalize_by_sum(v)
    t = sum(v)
    return map(x -> x / t, v)
end

@testset "inference" begin
    v = rand(16)
    @inferred normalize_by_sum(v)
    r = normalize_by_sum(v)          # warm up
    @test eltype(r) == Float64
end

function boxed_variant(v)
    t = 0.0
    t = sum(v)
    return map(x -> x / t, v)
end

has_box(f, argtypes) = occursin("Core.Box", sprint(code_warntype, f, argtypes))
has_box(normalize_by_sum, (Vector{Float64},))   # → false
has_box(boxed_variant, (Vector{Float64},))      # → true
```

`@test !has_box(f, argtypes)` makes the "no captured-variable box" property explicit. Remember `@inferred` only checks the call it is given; add one per supported argument type.

`M[:, j] = v .* j` first builds `v .* j` as a temporary and then copies it into the column. Broadcast with `.=` and the right side is fused straight into the slice:

```julia
function fill_cols!(M, v)
    for j in axes(M, 2)
        M[:, j] .= v .* j          # fused, writes into the column, no temporary
    end
    M
end

function fill_cols_temp!(M, v)
    for j in axes(M, 2)
        M[:, j] = v .* j
    end
    M
end

M = zeros(200, 200); v = rand(200)
fill_cols!(M, v); fill_cols_temp!(M, v)
@allocated fill_cols!(M, v)         # → 0
@allocated fill_cols_temp!(M, v)    # → 332800
```

Remember `M[:, j] .= ...` assigns through a view of the column, so it is also the correct spelling when the left-hand side is a slice.

Not in a hot path. An allocation costs the allocation itself, memory traffic, and GC work proportional to how much garbage you make. And a function that allocates cannot be as fast as its in-place twin. Measure it for your case:

```julia
using BenchmarkTools

add_new(a, b) = a .+ b
add_inplace!(c, a, b) = (c .= a .+ b)

a, b = rand(10^4), rand(10^4); c = similar(a)
@btime add_new($a, $b);
@btime add_inplace!($c, $a, $b);
```

The in-place version avoids the allocation; the benchmark output's "allocations" column shows `0 allocations` for it and a 78 KiB result array for the other. How much time that is depends on the size, since small arrays use a fast allocation path and large ones put pressure on the GC.

The practical rule: a one-off allocation in setup code is fine. An allocation inside an inner loop, or one proportional to the problem size per iteration, is worth removing, and `@allocated` / `@allocations` are how you find out.

Three allocations are not a problem in themselves; what matters is whether the number scales with the problem size or the number of iterations, and how often the function runs. Test that directly:

```julia
function build(n)
    out = Vector{Float64}(undef, n)
    for i in eachindex(out)
        out[i] = i
    end
    out
end

function build_slow(n)
    out = Float64[]
    for i in 1:n
        push!(out, i)
    end
    out
end

build(10); build_slow(10)
@allocations build(10)              # → 2
@allocations build(10_000)          # → 3
@allocations build_slow(10)         # → 4
@allocations build_slow(10_000)     # → 15
```

A count that stays at a handful as `n` grows (the output array and its wrapper) is fine unless the function is called millions of times in an inner loop; then pass the output buffer in. A count that grows with `n` or with iterations means an allocation inside the loop. Also check bytes (`@allocated`), since one large allocation is more expensive than three small ones.
